hey folks welcome to reality check the  once was an idea that was the gamer a  shut-in  a recluse a person forced to look to  digital interaction to really fit in and  to find meaning in society more than  just that though I'm sure we've all  heard the mainstream stereotype that  online gamers used digital worlds as an  analogue or replacement for real-life  interactions and that these scenarios  make it easier for the socially  maladjusted to converse with others  without some of the messiness or  friction of real-life engagement now you  and I as people who play games we all  have a wealth of anecdotal observations  for why this just isn't true  unfortunately though this notion does  persist amongst certain groups of  society well thankfully we now have some  hard science we can show these people  and help disprove this myth once and for  all and that's what this week's reality  check is all about  public displays of play studying online  games in physical settings is a study  headed up by researchers at North  Carolina State University and the paper  published in the Journal of computer  mediated communication the researchers  revealed that online gaming can  supplement or even enhance gamers social  lives rather than inhibiting them in the  words of lead author dr. Nick Taylor  gamers aren't the antisocial basement  dwellers we see in pop culture  stereotypes they're highly social people  loners are the outliers in gaming not  the norm so how did they demonstrate  this while the researchers analyzed the  behavior of thousands of online gamers  most notably those who play MMOs like  world Warcraft and EVE Online they  attended a large number of different  industry events in Canada and the UK  now these included large and small land  parties fan conventions internet cafes  and pub based gaming nights alongside  these large observations and interviews  the researchers also carried out a  smaller scale but in-depth survey of 378  gamers focusing on how they communicated  with others their goal was to track both  the online and offline behavior of  gamers focusing on how they communicated  with one another their overwhelmingly  they observed that gaming was just one  aspect of social behavior going on at  these events once again in the words of  dr. Taylor we find that gamers were  often exhibiting many social behaviors  at once watching games talking drinking  and chatting online now while some of  the settings observed where gamers  played such as internet cafes with  segregated cubicles did discourage  offline interaction this was very much  in the minority  conversely local area network or LAN  parties gaming conventions and pub based  gaming meetups all supplemented or  enhanced gamers overall social  connectedness in the study the example  of LAN parties was highlighted  explaining that these were most often  played with friends romantic partners or  guild mates so for most people the  purpose of being there was to take part  in both the online and offline  interactions interestingly this was true  regardless of which games gamers are  actually playing and all  regardless of how they behaved in-game  so if they're playing in a heavily  adversarial way this did not appear to  translate to the real-world interactions  a player could be utterly ruthless in  the game and still socialize normally  offline this ties in nicely to another  thing said by the research team now they  make the case that what goes on in an  MMO should not be thought of as an  analog or an alternative to what goes on  in real life they argue that we  shouldn't think of players as either  being here in the real world or there  online but rather that one is meshed  with in the other in essence remember  that it is just a game and that the vast  majority of people who play online games  maintain that sense of perspective one  thing dr. Taylor and the rest of the  team did note however is that this work  focused on Western gaming community  specifically and that there could well  be social differences and therefore  different results were this test to be  replicated in other gaming cultures and  that this is something they'd like to  look into in the future so what did you  make of this study let me know in the  comments also if you'd like to have a  read of the paper yourself then head on  over to my blog which is tinyurl.com  /cam france blog and they'll be a link  there alternatively if you're watching  on youtube then that link is also in the  description  last week on reality check I did a  call-out for potential future topics and  I got a ton of great suggestions  especially over Twitter user Levy Smith  suggested you should do a reality check  episode of Batman and if his gadgets are  possible he'll - Rocksteady Bill Hudson  asked what causes a civilization just  one more turn mentality and Atsuto  Kieran said do shields like in Star Trek  or Halo or Thor or any sci-fi thing  thank you very much for those ideas guys  I've added them all to the list now if  you have a suggestion or would like to  get in touch with me about anything else  then just hit me up on Twitter at kam  fries Rob thanks for watching  you
 

